# A shared library that hooks a function the moment it is loaded — no host code
# has to ask it to. This is the whole native-interop story in one file: a `dll`
# to call into C, `address of` to be called back by C, and `dll_attach` to run
# under the OS loader (DllMain on Windows, an ELF constructor on Linux).
#
# It is built to `libhook.so` / `hook.dll`. The `hookrt` library beside it owns
# a function dispatched through a slot; loading this library installs a detour
# into that slot, so the host's very next call through it lands here instead.
module hook
target sharedlib

# The detour runtime this library reaches into: the original behind a trampoline,
# and the installer that redirects the slot. Bound at the first call, which is
# inside `dll_attach`.
dll hookrt_original(x: int): int from "hookrt"
dll hookrt_install(detour: ptr) from "hookrt"

# The detour: C's slot points here after the hook is installed, so a call the
# host makes through `hookrt_call` arrives in this subroutine. It reaches the
# original through the trampoline and adds one, so a hooked call is observably
# different from an un-hooked one — proof that target, detour and trampoline all
# round-trip.
sub detour(x: int): int
  return hookrt_original(x) + 1
end

# The loader hook. It runs when the OS maps this library — from DllMain's
# DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH on Windows, from a constructor on Linux — before any host
# code has a chance to call in. It does one small thing: hand C the address of
# `detour` to install. Work heavier than this belongs on a thread spawned here,
# because a loader hook runs under the OS loader lock.
sub dll_attach
  call hookrt_install(address of detour)
end
